## What I found

Measurement, not guessing: a bare `TabBar` hints 25px, but installing the per-tab close `QToolButton` stretched every tab to 30px — Qt's `tabSizeHint` computes `max(textHeight, widget->size()) + style vframe`, so the button's ~19px was feeding the whole strip. On a real desktop font/style that lands well past the ~30-32px browser norm.

## The fix (3 surgical changes in `src/tabbar.cpp`)

- **`installCloseButton`** — fixed the button at `qBound(12, fm.height(), 16)` so it can never contribute height; still vertically centered in the tab rect.
- **`tabSizeHint`** — kept the UIP01 floor (`fm + 10`) and added a cap: `qBound(fm+10, natural, max(fm, iconSize)+12)` — icon+text+padding on any style. Vertical-mode rows keep their one-text-line height (the bounded value is meaningless against transposed hints).
- **Latent bug** — `containerChipSize()` returned an invalid `QSize()` (−1,−1) for the no-chip cases, silently shaving 1px at every `+ chip.height()` site; now returns `QSize(0,0)`.

## Verified

- Real `BrowserMainWindow` strip: **30 → 27px** (fm=15), offscreen grab reviewed — text centered, ~8px padding/side, close button + favicon centered.
- New `tst_TabBar::tabStripHeight` locks the bound formula, the widget cap, and button centering.
- `tst_tabbar` 11/11, `tst_tabwidget` 18/18, `tst_containermanager` 34/34, `tst_browsermainwindow` 26/26, `--tabstrip-smoke` 21/21.
