## What happened

**Investigation (option a → b):** No per-window title-bar tint exists on this GNOME Wayland host — Qt 6.12's Adwaita client-side decoration only reads the session-wide `org.freedesktop.appearance color-scheme` portal key, and `_GTK_THEME_VARIANT` is X11-only. So the spec's fallback (b) was built.

**Design:** Tor windows (non-macOS) go `Qt::FramelessWindowHint` and the menubar row itself doubles as a drawn title bar, headerbar-style:

- `QMenuBar::setCornerWidget` hosts `TorTitleBar` on the left (Tor badge + live window title + drag surface) and painted min/max/close glyphs on the right
- Empty menubar space drags via `QWindow::startSystemMove` (manual fallback offscreen, same as PipWindow), double-click toggles maximize, right-click opens a window menu
- A violet frame ring (`contentsMargins`) doubles as the `startSystemResize` grab zone and collapses to 0 while maximized/fullscreen
- Normal windows keep native decoration untouched

**Key debug find:** the earlier `setMenuWidget` wrapper approach is fundamentally broken — the next `menuBar()` call lazily creates a fresh *empty* QMenuBar when the slot doesn't hold one, silently deleting the wrapper and losing all menus. Corner widgets on the real menubar avoid this entirely.

**Verification:**
- `tst_BrowserMainWindow::torTitleBar` + full suite: **36/36 PASS**
- Offscreen grab: violet title row + frame ring confirmed visually
- `--tor-window-smoke` on real tor: **PASS** (IsTor:true on NAM + WebEngine, drawn title bar asserted)
- `make check`: **0 failed across all programs**, check-warnings PASS

Bookkeeping: task row → `done` with details-section writeup, ChangeLog bullet, WORKLOG line. Stopping here — not starting the next row.
