**What the user actually saw:** the row *geometry* was never the problem — measured `tabRect`s on a real window were already `fm+8` (25px vs the horizontal strip's 26), `expanding=false` via `documentMode`. The real defect was a Qt 6.12 Fusion-style bug in `CE_TabBarTabShape`: for the selected tab it calls

```cpp
fillRect(rect.left()+1, rect.bottom()-1, rect.width()-2, rect.bottom()-1, tabFrameColor)
```

— the height argument gets the rect's absolute bottom coordinate, so a tab-colored ghost band `(bottom-1)`px deep paints below the selected row. On a 26px strip it lands outside the widget; on a ~600px vertical rail the selected row grows a block proportional to its depth — the "roomy padding" in the screenshot. It only fires for `HasFrame` tabs (real `QTabWidget` parent), which is why standalone-bar unit tests could never see it — confirmed by grab bisection (block survives a magenta prefill, vanishes when tab draws are skipped, no child widget covers it).

**Fix:** `painter.setClipRect(option.rect)` around the two hand-painted `CE_TabBarTab` calls in the vertical branch (rows + floating drag row). The CONT06 banded *horizontal* path stays unclipped — same convention as `QTabBar::paintEvent`, and clipping would eat Fusion's intentional `tabOverlap` wing.

