## What was wrong

The daemon spawn path itself worked in-process, so the real-world failure was environmental: the expert-bundle `tor` binary has **no RPATH** — its libssl/libcrypto/libevent sit beside the executable. Under an inherited loader path (bundle wrapper's `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`, sandbox runtimes) the child binds incompatible libs and dies at exec with no diagnostics. Any pre-bootstrap exit then immediately declared failure, stranding the window on the fail-closed proxy — every tab showing raw `ERR_PROXY_CONNECTION_FAILED`.

## What I changed

- **`tormanager.cpp/h`** — prepend the resolved binary's dir to the child's `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` (`DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH` on macOS); `retryOrFail()` respawns up to 2× at 1.5s on early exit/launch error, cancelled by `stop()`, budget reset on Ready/manual restart.
- **`torstatusbanner.*` (new)** — honest-state strip above the tabs: "Connecting to the Tor network — N%" while bootstrapping, "Tor is unavailable: reason" + Retry on failure, hidden once Ready.
- **`browsermainwindow.cpp/h`** — hosts the banner in tor windows only.
- **`browserapplication.cpp`** — tor notice lines now reach `qWarning`; failed `startDetached` warns.
- **Fail-closed preserved** — proxy stays `127.0.0.1:1` until real SOCKS; no clearnet fallback anywhere.

## Verified

